diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index 4598debfc..2cfef2e01 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -88,6 +88,12 @@ Each release's page on GitHub has its notes and measurements. See [the 1.0.0 rel
 
 - HIP: the load-time lane cost table times a shared bucket of two or more windows with a distinct token a row, as shared rounds draft, instead of one repeated token; lone windows, one-row buckets, the head step and one-stream servers are timed as before, and only the drafted width can change, never a token (#2652, part of #2605). Thanks to @AbrahamCete.
 
+- Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on CUDA: `--kv-cache fp8` (one rank, our glue; not with `--slide`, a captured set, the KV pages or
+  `--learn-conversations`) keeps each position's keys and values as e4m3 codes with a power-of-two scale a (position, KV
+  head) row, 1,056 bytes a position and layer instead of 2,048, so a token's caches take about 17.5 KiB instead of 30 KiB
+  and a Spark's sequence memory admits five full-window streams where bf16 admits three. The indexer's keys, pooled blocks
+  and DeltaNet states stay bf16; attention reads the codes widened to bf16 exactly, so its sums are the bf16 kernels' on
+  the dequantized rows, and a repeated prompt's reply is deterministic. bf16 stays the default (#9999).
 - Tool calls: a reply's call ids are an HMAC-SHA-256 of the request (its body without `stream` and `stream_options`, the choice and the call's index) under the server's key instead of chance, so a request replayed to the same server gets the same ids, a stream names its calls as its whole reply and the next turn's background prefill do, and a Responses `fc_` item id follows its call; without the key an id reveals nothing about the request, and byte-identical requests under one key share ids. The key is random per server process unless `TENSORFOLD_TOOL_ID_KEY_FILE` names an owner-only file of at least 32 bytes, which keeps ids across restarts and machines sharing it, and a missing, unreadable, linked, shared, short or oversized file stops the start by name; the `call_` and 24 hex digit form and the ids a client sends back are unchanged (#2653).
 
 - Qwen3.8-27B on Metal: after a sampled or grammar-masked verify, only the drafter's taps and head row are waited for; the target's keep follows on the same queue and is settled before the next verify, so the drafter starts sooner, with every token unchanged. One stream sampled with DFlash2 on an M3 Ultra: chat 75.3 to 77.0 tok/s, code 106.8 to 109.8 (#2646). Thanks to @mrv777.
diff --git a/docs/recipes/flash-next-cuda.md b/docs/recipes/flash-next-cuda.md
index dd413c25a..4e400e5c5 100644
--- a/docs/recipes/flash-next-cuda.md
+++ b/docs/recipes/flash-next-cuda.md
@@ -79,6 +79,12 @@ tensorfold-native serve /path/to/checkpoint --temperature 0
   layer reads 512 blocks of 4 keys a row, chosen by the model's block indexer, as the Python engine does.
 - `--parallel N` admits N streams. Each stream's caches are its own: about 110 MiB of DeltaNet state and the n-gram
   tail, plus 28 KiB a token of keys, values and indexer keys, and with drafts its MTP head's cache.
+- `--kv-cache fp8` (one rank, the engine's own glue; not with `--slide`, a captured set, the KV pages or
+  `--learn-conversations`) keeps keys and values as e4m3 codes with a power-of-two scale a position and KV head: 1,056 bytes
+  a position and layer instead of 2,048, so a token's caches take about 17.5 KiB instead of 30 KiB (12 layers and the MTP
+  head's). The indexer's keys, the pooled blocks and the DeltaNet states stay bf16. Attention reads the codes widened to
+  bf16 exactly, so it is the bf16 kernels' arithmetic on rows rounded to e4m3: lossy against a bf16 cache, deterministic
+  for a given prompt. bf16 stays the default.
 - `--no-drafts` serves without the MTP head; `"draft": false` on a request does the same for that request.
 - `TF_FLASHNEXT_READOUT_ROWS` (default 64, `0` never) is the rows from which the hyper-connection read-out takes the prompt
   tiles. Same bits either way; `128` keeps 16-stream decode rounds on the row kernels, up to 7% quicker on a GB10.
